      ],
      "basic_overview": "The Cannizzaro Reaction is a base-catalyzed disproportionation (redox) reaction of non-enolizable aldehydes (aldehydes lacking $\\alpha$-hydrogens) into an equal mixture of a primary alcohol (reduction product) and a carboxylic acid salt/carboxylate (oxidation product). The reaction proceeds via nucleophilic attack of hydroxide on a carbonyl carbon followed by a rate-determining intramolecular or intermolecular hydride transfer to a second aldehyde molecule.",
      "has_extra_info": true,
      "extra_info_btn_color": "#2563eb",
      "extra_info_content": "1. Dependence on Base Concentration: At moderate base concentration ($[OH^-]$), the rate law is second-order in aldehyde and first-order in base ($Rate = k_1 [RCHO]^2 [OH^-]$) via the mono-anion intermediate. At very high base concentrations, a di-anion intermediate forms ($[R-C(O^-)_2H]$), shifting the kinetics to second-order in base ($Rate = k_2 [RCHO]^2 [OH^-]^2$). 2. Crossed Cannizzaro Reaction: When formaldehyde ($HCHO$) is reacted with another non-enolizable aldehyde (e.g., $PhCHO$), formaldehyde is preferentially oxidized to formate ($HCOO^-$) because its carbonyl carbon is extraordinarily electrophilic, driving quantitative reduction of the aromatic/complex aldehyde to its corresponding benzyl alcohol.",
      "examples": "1. Self-Disproportionation: Benzaldehyde ($2 \\text{ PhCHO} \\xrightarrow{50\\% \\text{ NaOH, } \\Delta} \\text{PhCH}_2\\text{OH} + \\text{PhCOO}^-\\text{Na}^+$). 2. Crossed Cannizzaro: Benzaldehyde + Formaldehyde ($PhCHO + HCHO \\xrightarrow{\\text{NaOH}} PhCH_2OH + HCOO^-\\text{Na}^+$). 3. Intramolecular Cannizzaro: Phenylglyoxal ($Ph-CO-CHO \\xrightarrow{\\text{OH}^-} Ph-CH(OH)-COO^-$, Mandellate anion) or Glyoxal ($OCH-CHO \\xrightarrow{\\text{OH}^-} HO-CH_2-COO^-$). 4. Steric Acceleration: Trimethylacetaldehyde ($(CH_3)_3CCHO \\xrightarrow{\\text{OH}^-} (CH_3)_3CCH_2OH + (CH_3)_3CCOO^-$).",
      "conditions_and_medium": "1. Standard Conditions: Concentrated aqueous or alcoholic base ($30\\% - 50\\% \\text{ NaOH}$ or $KOH$), elevated temperature ($50 - 100^\\circ\\text{C}$). 2. Solvents: Water, ethanol-water mixtures, or phase-transfer catalysts ($PTC$) for hydrophobic aromatic aldehydes. 3. Substrate Scope: Aldehydes with no $\\alpha$-hydrogens ($PhCHO$, $HCHO$, $ArCHO$, $R_3CCHO$, furfural).",
      "mechanism_steps": "Step 1 (Reversible Addition): Hydroxide anion attacks the electron-deficient carbonyl carbon of the aldehyde to form a tetrahedral mono-anion intermediate. Step 2 (Rate-Determining Hydride Transfer): The unshared electron pair on oxygen collapses to regenerate the $C=O$ double bond, simultaneously expelling a hydride ion ($H^-$) directly onto the carbonyl carbon of a second aldehyde molecule. Step 3 (Proton Transfer): Rapid, thermodynamically favorable proton transfer from the newly formed carboxylic acid ($pK_a \\sim 4-5$) to the alkoxide ion ($pK_a \\sim 16$) yields the stable carboxylate salt and primary alcohol.",
      "kinetics_and_energy": "The transition state of Step 2 involves a concerted 6-membered cyclic electron shift (or direct 1,3-hydride delivery) requiring high activation energy ($E_a \\sim 18-22 \\text{ kcal/mol}$). The driving force for the reaction is the irreversible acid-base neutralization step in Step 3 ($pK_a$ gap of $\\sim 11$ units, $\\Delta G^\\circ \\approx -15 \\text{ kcal/mol}$).",
      "selectivity_and_stereochemistry": "1. Chemoselectivity: Non-enolizable aldehydes exclusively undergo Cannizzaro under strong base. If $\\alpha$-hydrogens are present, the Aldol Condensation proceeds exponentially faster due to rapid proton abstraction ($pK_a \\sim 19$). 2. Regioselectivity in Crossed Cannizzaro: Sterically unhindered, highly electrophilic aldehydes (like formaldehyde) undergo preferred attack by $OH^-$ and act strictly as the hydride donor (oxidized partner).",
      "common_traps": "1. Attempting Cannizzaro on aldehydes with $\\alpha$-hydrogens (e.g., $CH_3CHO$, $CH_3CH_2CHO$), which undergo polymerization/aldol condensation instead. 2. Misidentifying the oxidized product in a Crossed Cannizzaro with formaldehyde ($HCHO$ is ALWAYS oxidized to $HCOO^-$, never reduced). 3. Forgetting that all LaTeX backslashes inside JSON string values must be strictly double-escaped (`\\\\`).",
